592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1031 "The Determinant of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1033 152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049044 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d58eda6d-18a9-51fc-a2ae-3720429fd25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1031 "Area Scale Factors of Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1033 989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049339 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ecea026a-a4d7-5bbd-a8a4-2897be68c279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Transformations of Objects in the Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1033 866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049420 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f351f883-c87d-5a54-90d7-d8ba977cd2e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179428 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179428 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2a/2abec0420bf322120bb37135af682a7bc04c97c86d7bf1bdfd04012a8355883c.png)\n\nConsider the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ above, and the transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 7, \\qquad y = u + v +1.\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\overline{PQ}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the segment $\\overline{P'Q'}$ in the $xy$-plane. Find $\\overline{P'Q'}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "102d79a9-f19c-5735-a3f2-347938820b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179428 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+7 \\\\ u+v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overline{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overline{P'Q'}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overline{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n![](images/8c/8c18a13c717349bccc5af673ee81a9cdc56f9f2a5c32ce75dc8e63fe91765b1e.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/16/16c730f4e70bed4d3c8bd9840b457a3cfdad3905b0ada078e71044b73f3f2800.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $\\mathbf T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -10 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(0,-2)$ and $(-3,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/61/61508357c514f50b27498bee50dce36d22b7f55f0baf8c2d6a58ad2d53dc879d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179429 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179429 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dd/dd8b1407add93e72afccf2138cc818df63d47fdaa6ccc23e843a5602d8c1dbd8.png)\n\n          \n\nConsider the rectangle $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: -1\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx = u - v + 1, \\qquad y = u+v\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "290ea83a-1a47-5260-82b2-346d38e9adcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179429 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v+1 \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $\\mathbf T$ maps the boundary of $\\Delta$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $\\Delta,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/69/69d92e24d5b0c81e54d3878793efe7c07d16fc963222997fe4fec4ffb3f93bd5.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n![](images/18/1811ded1ce67ec4ebb443816fdf3eb0080b84c6f84852a04b9b66b4e3df35de6.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/11/1110f6582a0a398accdf5a7cda1cd9676e79ecc251fa5ef2c080fc19867e0096.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(1,-2), (3,0), (1,2),$ and $(-1,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/dd/ddd14bdebe21c830396e044721811cf6b9c7bd7db067e4d0ff7d35e02a592894.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that since $\\det(A) = 2 >    0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179430 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179430 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = v - u-6, \\qquad y = v -2u  .\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/13/135271f9b5fd7e9883b49d02ab2abcb2ed268081e887892e5c251c27de1e85de.svg)\n\n- $\\det(A) = 1$\n\n- The transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179430 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f180b16-c807-555d-b92c-826a216a31e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179430 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-u-6 \\\\ v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/dc/dc6ad7fdd5273aa2af71efc5963804edb118fee37596c36a6be3bd30b62467cc.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/42/4266dd2d8188293cb54af9bd8431e27b5ec6e5d936f56363fd692b20b0735e74.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\det(A) > 0,$ the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179434 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nx = 3u+4, \\qquad y = 4v - 2u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the area scale factor of the transformation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6f17219-642b-51d3-bebe-61519ee6651f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179434 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+4 \\\\ 4v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u \\\\ -2u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $\\mathbf T$ is ![](images/78/783a9503543c1305d982dd65a5f3929398f51db97442606bc03f96484aa03e58.svg)\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n![](images/75/7582ffa6b92e0cba1d8e303231754cfa98a30cfe242d7a6744442462102a02ed.svg)\n\nSo, the area scale factor of the transformation is\n\n$$\n|\\det(A)| = |12| =12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179767 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179767 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049420 #edb/ref 1