b/ref 17592186257291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186257705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259456 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f465cacf-0fa4-5950-9818-e0e076a19708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186257969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186258421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186258421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259467 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f795437e-9943-5a42-b8f8-60ebc080c6fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+3v-5,\\,y=2v+u-4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bc55bfe-e692-5546-9ff3-60dd10f588d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259663 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v-5 \\\\ 2v+u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=-2v+1,\\,y=4u-v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-4$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76a65107-91d8-5259-9f73-6e2fee8f6ae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259668 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v+1 \\\\ 4u-v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}|=0\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/43/43eb119ef43e136682d4451e4f85f007c63568f7a34880c46f87ab45122f5dd0.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=2-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d4da39e-0907-5e10-8ce6-cd308b5a0917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186259689 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-u \\\\ 2-v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/a8/a8a13c579c3ca8cc32f997c48e237c38d449aa07040de658589fa5cc68a02c55.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(2,2),(-1,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/14/146f9f36deeac0f3a7108a173485eb6d0d03138d18998a96dc9c3566bee21ca2.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=1>0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371708 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/be/be30ef880c451fec095190537577b09896aa6b849e99b90f8185501ee0ef1296.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u-v-1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d30c881c-58fe-5ecb-bbb8-134fe86398ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371707 #edb/ref 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u-v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/06/06ceacb05656e99e545561e5cd5733529a1cf7f052beb6086b2fd42667041713.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-2),(1,0),(2,-1),$ and $(0,-3).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/92/92b9079151925202a5346e6b1dd2082b21a4e4102720673773e958c5f3644c4f.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-2<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371708 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "662f580a-3722-5158-b5a5-982aa